ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p3-2 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 3 Paragraph 2" ;
    doc:bbox "58.35655212402344,72.33061218261719,296.87091064453125,172.65806579589844" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-9 ;
    doc:pageNumber 3 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 2 ;
    doc:text "a maintenance operation. Such request can be divided into the following three sub-queries as shown in Figure. 3. The ﬁrst query Q1 requests all the timeseries Ids of the temperature sensors on the second ﬂoor. The results of the ﬁrst query R1 returns the series id. Q2 takes the results (R1), the series id, performs a join with the timeseries database, then ﬁlters those having negative values. The returned results R2 represents the timeseries Ids having negative values. Q3 projects such Ids with the sensor Ids and the room location of the sensors." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/having%20negative>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/having%20negative%20values>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/ids>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/negative>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/results> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/3.>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Figure>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Ids>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Q1>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Q2>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Q3>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/R1>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/R2>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/d.>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/second>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/three> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-3 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p3-3 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 3 Paragraph 3" ;
    doc:bbox "314.3782043457031,129.38023376464844,552.8927001953125,172.91856384277344" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-9 ;
    doc:pageNumber 3 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 3 ;
    doc:text "Line 25 of Listing 1 depicts the join operation between the two sub-queries which is the series Id. Line 1 corresponds to (Q3) of Fig. 3, it expresses the projected returned elements: the sensor Id, its room location." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/expresses>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/expresses%20projected>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/line>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/line%20corresponds>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/line%20corresponds%20fig> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/Fig>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Listing%201>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Q3>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/two> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-3 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p3-4 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 3 Paragraph 4" ;
    doc:bbox "58.35655212402344,174.3961639404297,296.87091064453125,206.6179656982422" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-9 ;
    doc:pageNumber 3 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 4 ;
    doc:text "(d) Scalability: analytics applications require millions of historical records for their algorithms. Thus, the federated engine is expected to return huge amounts of data." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/engine%20expected%20return>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/expected%20return%20huge>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/historical>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/historical%20records>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/historical%20records%20algorithms> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/millions> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-3 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p3-5 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 3 Paragraph 5" ;
    doc:bbox "58.35655212402344,208.3560028076172,296.8708801269531,251.93565368652344" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-9 ;
    doc:pageNumber 3 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 5 ;
    doc:text "(e) Reasoning: the underlying triple store exposes the reasoning feature in its query APIs, thus, the federated query engine must be able to expose such feature and forward the reasoning request to the underlying triple store." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/feature>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/reasoning>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/underlying>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/underlying%20triple>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/underlying%20triple%20store> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-3 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p3-6 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 3 Paragraph 6" ;
    doc:bbox "314.3782043457031,174.66001892089844,552.8961791992188,252.27183532714844" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-9 ;
    doc:pageNumber 3 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 6 ;
    doc:text "The SPARQL Federated Query [12] does not provide any optimization recommendations to be applied among subqueries. In Listing 1, the ﬁrst sub-query returns timeseries Id. However, the second sub-query will return all the timeseries with negative values without any ﬁlter on the Ids. Thus, without any optimization strategy, such solution is not well suited to bridge between the IoT graph and timeseries data." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/iot%20graph>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/iot%20graph%20timeseries>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/optimization>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/sub-query>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/timeseries> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/12>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Federated%20Query>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Ids>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/In%20Listing>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/IoT>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Listing%201>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/The%20SPARQL%20Federated%20Query>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/second> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-3 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p3-7 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 3 Paragraph 7" ;
    doc:bbox "58.35655212402344,253.71507263183594,296.88031005859375,274.5377197265625" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-9 ;
    doc:pageNumber 3 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 7 ;
    doc:text "Based on these requirements, we depict in the following the selected query language and the design of our engine." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/depict%20following>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/depict%20following%20selected>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/following%20selected>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/language%20design>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/language%20design%20engine> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-3 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p3-8 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 3 Paragraph 8" ;
    doc:bbox "314.3782043457031,254.01341247558594,552.892822265625,297.5516662597656" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-9 ;
    doc:pageNumber 3 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 8 ;
    doc:text "We discuss next our solution relying on an optimised query execution strategy based on query rewriting. Our proposed approach fulﬁll the requirements depicted in section III-A to federate two different types of data stores in a scalable manner." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/data%20stores%20scalable>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/execution%20strategy%20based>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/federate>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/federate%20different>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/federate%20different%20types> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/III-A>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/two> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-3 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p3-9 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 3 Paragraph 9" ;
    doc:bbox "58.35655212402344,296.3636474609375,296.8802795410156,362.61761474609375" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-9 ;
    doc:pageNumber 3 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 9 ;
    doc:text "In [12], the W3C published a SPARQL Federated Query recommendation allowing data consumers to query data distributed across the Web. This extension enables a query to have several sub-queries which can be directed to several SPARQL endpoints. The federated query processor recieves and combines the returned results." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/data%20consumers>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/data%20consumers%20query>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/data%20distributed>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/federated%20query>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/query> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/12>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Federated%20Query>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/W3C> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-3 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p4-1 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 4 Paragraph 1" ;
    doc:bbox "314.1884460449219,72.33073425292969,552.703125,104.51122283935547" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-12 ;
    doc:pageNumber 4 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 1 ;
    doc:text "ontology (respectively timeseries) store. It also performs join operations between two or more returned results from the subqueries according to the global ﬁlter expression." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/join%20operations>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/join%20operations%20returned>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/performs>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/performs%20join>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/subqueries> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/two> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-4 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p4-10 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 4 Paragraph 10" ;
    doc:bbox "314.1911926269531,328.09466552734375,552.7152099609375,544.6856689453125" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-12 ;
    doc:pageNumber 4 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 10 ;
    doc:text "Fig. 5. FOrT´E Pseudo Sequential Operations First FOrT´E receives a federated query such as Listing 1 from the application (1). Second, the planer passes (2) the query to the SPARQL Parser module which extracts the necessary elements as depicted previously. Third, the planer forwards (3) the triple store sub-queries to the Federated Executor which executes (4) them and waits for results. By combining the returned SPARQL results (5), the global ﬁlter expression and the timeseries sub-query, lines 19-23, the planer requests (6) from the Writer am optimized SQL query, as shown in Listing 2. Later, the SQL query is passed (7) to the Federated Executor which executes it (8) through the SparkSQL module on the timeseries storage. The returned timeseries results are then joined (9) with the SPARQL results of step (4). Furthermore, based on the global select operation, a projection is applied (10) to return the requested variables only, as shown in Listing 3. Additionally, the results of (10) are returned (11) to the planer which returns (12) the response format according to the one requested such as CSV [11] or JSON [10]." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/executor%20executes>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/federated%20executor%20executes>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/planer>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/results>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/sparql%20results> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/10>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/11>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/12>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/19-23>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/8%29>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/CSV>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Federated%20Executor>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/JSON>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Listing%201>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Listing%202.>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Listing%203>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Pseudo%20Sequential%20Operations%20First>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL%20Parser>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SQL>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Second>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SparkSQL>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Third>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/the%20Federated%20Executor> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-4 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p4-11 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 4 Paragraph 11" ;
    doc:bbox "314.1911926269531,546.3704223632812,552.7056884765625,567.193115234375" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-12 ;
    doc:pageNumber 4 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 11 ;
    doc:text "In the following section, we go through the implementation choices then the performance evaluation." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/choices%20performance>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/choices%20performance%20evaluation>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/following%20section>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/following%20section%20implementation>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/implementation%20choices> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-4 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p4-12 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 4 Paragraph 12" ;
    doc:bbox "58.006343841552734,540.6420288085938,296.5206604003906,629.611572265625" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-12 ;
    doc:pageNumber 4 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 12 ;
    doc:text "The SPARQL-SQL writer receives additional parameters to be injected such as the list of timeseries Ids from the sub-query executed ﬁrst on the triple store, lines 3-17, Listing 1. Then, this module generates at least two optimized SQL queries, the ﬁrst, shown in Listing 2, will be executed on the timeseries store. the second query is the transformation of the SPARQL global select, Listing 1-line 1, and the global ﬁlter, Listing 1line 25, into a SQL query as shown in Listing 3." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/executed>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/global>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/listing>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/shown%20listing>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/store> ;
    dom:mentionsEntity <http://example.org/resource/entity/Ids>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Listing%201-line>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Listing%201line%2025>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Listing%202>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/Listing%203>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SPARQL-SQL>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/SQL>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/at%20least%20two>,
        <http://example.org/resource/entity/second> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-page-4 .

ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-p4-13 a doc:Paragraph ;
    rdfs:label "Page 4 Paragraph 13" ;
    doc:bbox "314.1911926269531,574.2349243164062,552.7151489257812,643.2550048828125" ;
    doc:inSection ex:fort-a-federated-ontology-and-timeseries-query-engine-section-12 ;
    doc:pageNumber 4 ;
    doc:paragraphOrder 13 ;
    doc:text "IV. IMPLEMENTATION FOrT´E is implemented as a software component in Java, it connects to any triple store exposing a standard SPARQL endpoint and a timeseries store with a suitable JDBC SparkSQL compatible connector. The engine exposes a REST service by relying on nanohttpd web server." ;
    dom:hasTopic <http://example.org/resource/topic/engine%20exposes%20rest>,
        <http://example.org/resource/topic/iv%20implementation>,
